  "latest_final": "The student was not actually choosing academic classes; \"courses\" were the courses of a meal. While dining out one evening (with the wording about a course-selection deadline being a misleading frame), the student did something at the table—an action the waiter responded to—and the waiter, believing he was being helpful, deliberately cleared away the plates, taking the physical dishes. What the student perceived as \"all my courses were gone in one evening\" was therefore not the loss of university enrollments at all, but the loss of the successive dishes of the meal. The removal was done in person, without any account credentials or special administrative privilege, and without malice: it was a well-intentioned waiter misreading the student's table behavior as a sign to clear everything. So the \"gone courses\" were simply the cleared dishes of the dinner.",
